function methodDialog(){
openDialog(`<h2>From game record<br>to alternate outcome.</h2><p>GoBlueBot is designed around <strong>Michigan’s demonstrated offense</strong>. This working interface contains all ${PLAYS.length} Michigan offensive snaps from the official 2026 play-by-play (${Object.keys(GAMES).length} games, updated after each game). A study only draws on the ${ELIGIBLE.length} snaps from games before its target. They are official statistical events, not verified complete play calls.</p><ol><li><strong>Collect prior Michigan games.</strong> Join official play-by-play, full-game film, rosters, participation records and any usable coaching or charting material.</li><li><strong>Reconstruct each call.</strong> Verify personnel, all-player alignment, motion, routes, blocking and quarterback reads. Keep ambiguous assignments as alternatives, not invented facts.</li><li><strong>Check applicability.</strong> Match the target personnel and available time. A substitution scenario lets the defense adjust and is compared separately.</li><li><strong>Rebuild Oregon’s look.</strong> Confirm pre-snap positions and use several plausible coverages when assignments are uncertain. Both modes permit movement after the snap.</li><li><strong>Simulate both calls.</strong> A validated model would produce yardage distributions, first-down probability and turnover risk. The original and alternate calls must use comparable execution assumptions.</li></ol><p class="dialog-callout">No model can observe the unplayed outcome of this exact snap. The useful claim is that one call appears better supported under explicit assumptions, with measured uncertainty.</p><h3>Two defensive comparisons</h3><p><strong>Same look:</strong> preserve the original pre-snap alignment while defenders react to the new play.<br><strong>Allow check:</strong> model a defensive adjustment to the new offensive formation, then simulate the snap.</p><h3>What works in this prototype</h3><p>Browse every verified snap of the season, filter by game, down and situation, search by player, see the observed conversion history for the filtered set, inspect each source and its official text, view the candidate Oregon event, switch comparison assumptions, import an image for local reference and export the study as JSON. The field camera changes the display.</p><h3>What is not built yet</h3><p>Computer vision, full formation reconstruction, reviewed play assignments, player tracking, calibrated forecasts and animated predicted outcomes. These remain locked rather than being filled with generic plays or invented probabilities.</p><h3>Research foundations</h3><p>Existing work on <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.17220" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NFL ghost defenders</a> and <a href="https://cmsac-research-lab.github.io/papers/nfl-step-turn/nfl-step-turn.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hypothetical ball-carrier movement</a> supports parts of the approach. It does not validate complete Michigan alternate-call predictions.</p>`, 'THE MODEL · DESIGN, NOT A TRAINED ENGINE');
}
